course: dictionary
lesson: 3572
title: Fete: a school fair and a big celebration
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A fete is a word you may meet at school, in the news or in a maths problem. Use the context to work it out. «The parents of both streets even ran a summer fete together, and argued only about the tombola.» Parents running something together, a summer event, and a tombola all point to a fair with stalls and games.
explain: The dictionary says a fete is an outdoor event, often at a school or church, with stalls, games and food to raise money. Check your meaning by swapping it in: «a summer party with stalls and games to raise money» still makes sense, so you have understood it.
explain: Fete is also a formal verb. «She was feted as a hero» means she was honoured with parties and praise. The same spelling can be a noun or a verb, so look at the sentence to see which job it does. Fête, with an accent, is another spelling of the same word.
fact: A fete (also spelled fête) is an outdoor event with stalls, games and food.
fact: Fetes are often held at schools or churches to raise money.
fact: The word is mostly used in the UK, Australia and New Zealand.
fact: «Feted» is a formal verb meaning to honour someone with parties and praise.
fact: Maths problems often use fete stalls: money raised, change and profit.
exercise: «The parents of both streets even ran a summer fete together.» What is a fete? | A quiet meeting in a library | *An outdoor event with stalls, games and food to raise money | A long journey by train | A kind of cake baked at home
exercise: At the school fete, a cake stall sold 40 cakes for 50p each. How much money did it raise? | £2 | £90 | *£20 | £4
exercise: «She was feted as a hero.» What does feted mean? | Ran away in fear | *Honoured with parties and praise | Sent home early | Forgotten by everyone
test: Lily says a fete is only for grown-ups and never happens at school. Is she right? | Yes, it is a type of shoe | No, it is a kind of race | *No, it is often held at a school or church, with stalls and games | Yes, it is a grown-up job only
test: The news said the team was «feted» after the win. What does that suggest? | They were forgotten | They were sent home | They lost badly | *They were praised and celebrated
exam: Mia's class fete made £60 from 4 stalls, and each stall made the same amount. How much did each stall make? | £12 | £240 | £20 | *£15
